      "writtenOn": "2022-02-28T15:43:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I have historically avoided conditionally defining an enumeration value to ensure consistency between builds with/without a given feature.\n\nSounds good, but as you said these values as used as index, so avoiding conditional defining would be problematic when we hit the limit of entries. Current solution works as long as new entries are added at the end of the enum and defines don\u0027t change between RO and RW.\n\n\u003e Is it worth an offline discussion of what could be done differently to prevent these types of bugs?\n\nI think we should discuss it when switching to Zephyr. Now I would leave it as it is.\n\n\u003e On possible design is to define a structure that is bit compatible with the existing RO data buffer.\n\nThere are many ROs with different structures. There is even difference between nocturne_fp and dartmonkey (the same hardware).\n\n\u003e Then devices with additional data(reset flags2, panic reason, etc) could wrap the RO data in a secondary structure that could be defined for each device type.\nSounds good. EC has a problem with panic data design - there is one panic data structure for all supported cores.",
      "parentUuid": "bc78c5ad_9a5ec19b",
      "revId": "8f69a5346ecd7f8997a2409b47e154adf07dcf42",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
